there's a lot going on but what have we  learned about Jim Comey from his book  and the interviews he's done about the  book well maybe the most shocking fact  of all is how banal he is come here and  the most powerful law-enforcement agency  in America he was a central player in  the last presidential election and in  its aftermath ever since you think he'd  be impressed when he speaks even if you  don't agree with him but no Jim Comey is  as conventional as they come we know  that now his interviews sound like  Chelsea Clinton doing a Q&amp;A with Us  Weekly Comey says his literary hero is  brace yourself Atticus Finch because  apparently couldn't think of anything  more cliched than that you couldn't  either he says he wishes Trump would  read the New York Times op-ed page he  recommended Sheryl Sandberg's lean in to  his staff at the FBI seriously and he  says he'd like to have Sandberg to a  dinner party and so on and on this is  America's top g-man or was he sounds  like every other sensitive guilt-ridden  middle-aged suburban liberal not  impressive at all maybe that's why Comey  did not hesitate before destroying any  remaining public trust in the FBI in  order to sell this book the FBI director  is not supposed to let emotion and  political considerations influence  government investigations but Comey  doesn't even hide it he admits his  behavior in the fall of 2016 may have  been colored by his desire to make  Hillary Clinton quote a legitimate  president so that settles that there's  now nobody in either party Republican or  Democrat who will ever again think the  Clinton investigation was conducted  fairly thanks Jim Comey or the Russia  investigation for that matter here's  what Comey told ABC about that watch did  you tell him that the steal does he had  been financed by his political opponents  no I didn't I didn't think I used the  term steal duskie I just talked about  additional material but did he have a  right to know that that had been  financed by his political opponents  that I don't know the answer to that it  wasn't necessary for my goal which is to  alert him that we had this information  telling the truth says Jim Comey quote  wasn't necessary for my goal apparently  it wasn't whatever else you think about  Donald Trump or his administration or  whatever you ought to be happy that that  guy Jim Comey is no longer running the  FBI Alan Dershowitz is a lawyer and  professor emeritus at Harvard Law School  as well as author of trumped up how  criminalization of political differences  in dangers democracy we spoke to him  just a minute ago  professor do the things that Comey has  said in written since he left the FBI  confirm the decision to fire him from  the FBI no it should have been done much  much earlier it should have been done on  the day of the inauguration and it  should have reflected itself in the fact  that nobody should ever have appointed  him to be head of the FBI this is a man  who was revenge driven who was prepared  to leak through a law professor at  Columbia who was prepared to disclose  confidential conversations he had with  the president-elect and the President of  the United States he's exactly the wrong  person to have headed the most important  law enforcement agency in the United  States this sends a horrible message to  FBI agents and law enforcement people  about leaking and about confidences and  about trying to keep emotion out of law  enforcement and politics out of law  enforcement to me the worst part of it  is when he both compares the president  to a mob boss which proves my point of  trumped up that you can create crimes  out of almost anything and call a mob  boss somebody who's running the  presidency of the United States and the  fact that he is willing to say on  television that it's possible that the  president was with prostitutes in a  hotel room with you know urination and  all that he's the head of the FBI he  knows the truth he has seen all the  secrets and for him to start speculating  on that kind of nonsense and rubbish and  not to disclose when he spoke to the  President as you said that this  information was paid for by a political  opponent you know I used to have  a good feeling about Comey I met him a  few times when he was in Boston I now  have only only very strong negative  feelings about him and he just enhances  the suspicion that I think so many  Americans now have of law enforcement  and we ought to be trusting law  enforcement and nobody today would trust  call me with secrets or confidences so  if he were alleging that the president  were acting like a mob boss you know  calling hits out on people committing  crimes that'd be one thing but he's in  effect criticizing the way the president  talks he's he's from Queens he does  actually sound like a mob boss sometimes  because he's from that part of the  country that's the kind of should law  enforcement officials be making comments  like this at all I mean doesn't it  detracts from the entire organization  that they purportedly represent and you  know the idea of revealing these secrets  it's is not 30 years later when he's  writing for history the way Henry  Kissinger is right and others have done  this is a gossipy revenge  make money quick get even hurt your  political opponents raised questions  about the politicizing of the Justice  Department and the FBI it's the worst  possible book at the worst possible time  for the worst possible reasons you know  we hear a lot about corrupt motives that  can't make a presidential act in write a  crime but talk about commis motives have  anybody asked him about his motives why  you wrote this book why does he feel  it's important for the American public  to hear about information that he has no  idea whether it's truthful or not it's  really scandalous and shameful and I'm  so disappointed with him so here's a lot  of friends in the FBI we know that  because the emails between them were  Fayed and now we have access to them  shouldn't it make us nervous because he  is now a political figure he's chosen to  become one what about you know Americans  who like Trump's should they be worried  about getting equal treatment from FBI  officials who are sympathetic to Jim  Comey well you know the problem is of  course it's not a problem  FBI agency human law enforcement people  are human they're are approached on  people in the FBI there and  from people they all take an oath not to  allow politics or personal views to get  in the way of their job and here we see  the former director letting his  political and personal I don't think  their partisan views there get even  views right get in the way of their of  their job and when you combine that with  the struct disclosures when you combine  that with some of the other disclosures  it may serve a good purpose because it  tells us the FBI agents are not what we  see in stereotypes in the movies they're  real human beings with passions but we  have to figure out a way of making it so  clear that these civil servants who  sacrifice their lives have to keep their  politics at home and their passions at  home and their personal views at home  and this just moves the conversation in  the wrong direction I agree and makes a  lot of people paranoid for good reason  professor thank you for coming on Razia

